Subject: [PATCH] F-P1-01 — call the prompt-injection guard on the read paths

Upstream: HKUDS/CLI-Anything, browser/agent-harness
Base commit: 34f519533bc175d2fe287ab8316b0dd99bb9cc43
Applies to: cli_anything/browser/utils/domshell_backend.py
Author: Steven Shearrill's engineering team (Security Engineer seat)
Date: 2026-09-22
Licence: Apache-2.0, as upstream. The modified file carries the
  Apache-2.0 section 4(b) "changed file" notice at the top.

Why
---
`cli_anything/browser/utils/security.sanitize_dom_text()` is this
package's own prompt-injection guard, and upstream's SECURITY.md names
prompt-injected agents as an explicit threat model — but nothing in
production ever called it. The only call sites were in
`cli_anything/browser/tests/test_security.py`. Every byte of DOM text
returned by `fs ls`, `fs cat` and `fs grep` reached the calling agent
unexamined. `validate_url()` was wired (core/page.py:40); the sanitizer
was not.

What
----
`_parse_execute_result()` is the single choke point every read path
funnels through — `ls`, `cat`, `grep`, `cd`, `click`, `type` and the
navigation commands all return through it, including the raw `fs` verbs
the homes / showingtime / showami harnesses re-expose. The guard is
called there, once, via a new `_guard_dom_text()`.

Deliberately NOT a drop-in call to `sanitize_dom_text()`. That function
replaces a flagged payload with a 200-character preview and truncates
anything over 10 KB. Silently discarding page content would hide from
the operator the very text they need to see, and would quietly break
`fs ls` on any large page. So the guard is used for its VERDICT and the
response is made loud instead of lossy:

  * the full page text is preserved, control characters scrubbed;
  * a `!! SECURITY:` banner is prepended to `output` / `raw`;
  * a structured `security` key is attached to the result dict, so
    `--json` consumers and orchestrators can hard-stop on it;
  * a WARNING goes to stderr through `logging`, which needs no CLI
    change to be visible.

Not changed: DOMShell's own error strings. `_is_error()` keys off the
ANSI red marker those carry, so error text is scanned but returned
byte-for-byte. `ls`/`grep` entry lists are not touched either —
downstream harnesses parse them positionally.

Evidence
--------
`integrations/cli-anything-harnesses/browser/tests/test_injection_guard_wired.py`
— 11 tests. 9 of them fail against the unpatched tree and all 11 pass
against the patched tree. Vendored suites: 622 passed / 42 skipped both
before and after.

Re-applying after an upstream re-sync
-------------------------------------
    cd integrations/cli-anything-harnesses/browser/agent-harness
    patch -p1 < ../patches/0001-wire-prompt-injection-guard-into-read-paths.patch
    python -m pytest ../tests ./cli_anything/browser/tests

